## Native contract (check before repairing)
- Backend-correct headers: kernel.mu is compiled by mcc (MUSA), so include MUSA
  runtime headers such as `<musa_runtime.h>`. CUDA-only headers (`<cuda.h>`,
  `<cuda_runtime.h>`) do not exist in this toolchain and abort the build; use
  them only when the target backend is CUDA, never in a MUSA file.
- Evaluation call convention: every consumer (the generated test, the
  deterministic case suite, and the KernelBench evaluator) invokes
  `kernel_function` through `bind_kernel_call`, which resolves each parameter
  in this priority: (1) BY NAME against the reference Model's state —
  named_parameters()/named_buffers() leaf names (e.g. `weight`, `bias`;
  aliases `w`, `gamma`, `beta`) and common module config attributes
  (`stride`, `padding`, `dilation`, `output_padding`, `groups`, `eps`,
  `num_groups`, `normalized_shape`, `kernel_size`); (2) otherwise POSITIONALLY
  with the tensors returned by `get_inputs()`, in order; (3) parameters with
  defaults are skipped. A required parameter that matches neither source fails
  every consumer with "Cannot bind required kernel parameter(s): <name>" —
  e.g. `kernel_function(input, dim)` fails because `dim` is fixed at
  reference-model construction, while `kernel_function(x, weight, bias)`
  binds weight and bias from the reference model by name. When the reference
  Model has parameters/buffers, declare them as explicit parameters named
  after the model state — their values live in the model instance and cannot
  be baked in; bake `get_init_inputs()` values into kernel.py (module constant
  or closure) instead of adding a required parameter.

Return the complete kernel.py, binding.cpp, kernel.mu, and setup.py using fenced
FILE blocks. Put each literal FILE: filename header outside and immediately
before its code fence. Return no prose and never copy reference Models, input
factories or tests into kernel.py. Do not repair a reference-test setup error by
changing native arithmetic; preserve all test semantics and tolerances.
